Chapter: ZigEmitHosted

 The plug as a program: IR in on stdin, zig source out. Same parser and
 same emitter as ZigPlugRing, with the serial ring and its SIZE: framing
 replaced by a read and a write, because a hosted process is handed its
 input by the shell rather than having to fetch it from a device.

 This is the piece that takes QEMU out of the loop. Today an IR reaches
 the emitter by booting a plug kernel and pushing 13MB through a gdbstub
 refill loop at roughly a third of a core's throughput; here it is a
 file read. The emitter's answer must not change, and that is the point
 of building it this way: the same ZigEmitter compiled two ways, and the
 ladder already knows how to insist they agree.

 The output goes to stderr, because print-text is cx_print and cx_print
 is std.debug.print. Redirect with 2> and it is a file. Putting it on
 stdout would want a builtin this plug does not have yet -- write-binary
 takes bytes and the emitter's answer is Text, so the conversion would
 have to happen in Codex rather than at the boundary.

Section: Body

  opening : [Console, FileSystem] Nothing = act
   src <- read-file-uni "/dev/stdin"
   let parsed = parse-ir-chapter src
   in print-text (emit-zig-chapter (parsed.chapter) (parsed.type-defs))
  end
